  📗
    Where the line that starts at *start* ends: the index of its CRLF, -1 when
    more bytes are needed, or -2 when the line holds a CR or an LF that is not
    part of a CRLF. Such a line is refused, not repaired: another parser may
    have ended it at the stray byte, and then the two disagree about every
    byte that follows.

    Each byte of a line is looked at once however many reads it arrives in,
    so a long line fed slowly costs time in proportion to its length.
